iree-rocket for Debian and Armbian
==================================

What the packages are
---------------------

One source package, iree-rocket, and four binary packages:

  rocket-compiler (amd64, arm64)
    /usr/bin/rocket-compiler                                Rust, dlopens:
    /usr/lib/<triplet>/rocket-compiler/libIREECompiler.so   IREE + LLVM +
                                                            the Rocket plugin
    /usr/lib/<triplet>/rocket-compiler/iree-lld             RUNPATH $ORIGIN
    /usr/share/rocket-compiler/rocket_conv2d_transform_spec.mlir

  libiree-rocket-runtime314 (arm64)
    /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libiree_rocket_runtime.so.314*

  libiree-rocket-runtime-dev (arm64)
    /usr/include/iree-rocket-runtime/{rocket,iree}/...
    /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libiree_rocket_runtime.{so,a}
    /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig/iree-rocket-runtime{,-static}.pc
    /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/cmake/IreeRocketRuntime/
    /usr/share/rocket-runtime/samples/{embed.cc,run_module.c}

  iree-rocket-tools (arm64)
    /usr/bin/rocket-run-module                links libiree-rocket-runtime314
    /usr/bin/iree-rocket-run-module           IREE's tools, Rocket driver
    /usr/bin/iree-rocket-benchmark-module     linked in statically

Why one source: a .vmfb only runs on a runtime with the IREE version and
rocket-schema it was compiled against, so the compiler and the runtime ship
from one upstream version.

The compiler's libIREECompiler.so is a private library on purpose. Its
SONAME is bare (`libIREECompiler.so`), it is a custom build carrying this
project's target plugin, and the transform spec only parses with the IREE it
was written against. So it lives in the package's own directory, and the
binary, library and spec always ship and upgrade together. rocket-compiler
finds them through absolute paths baked in at build time
(ROCKET_COMPILER_LIBDIR / ROCKET_COMPILER_DATADIR, read with option_env! in
rocket-compiler/src/main.rs).

The runtime library is public, and its SONAME follows IREE:
libiree_rocket_runtime.so.<major><minor, two digits>, so .so.314 for IREE
3.14. rocket-runtime/CMakeLists.txt derives it from IREE's
runtime/version.json. It exports IREE's whole C API (iree_*), which IREE does
not keep stable between releases, so every IREE bump is an ABI break and
renames the package. debian/rules fails the build when the SONAME and the
package name in debian/control disagree. Rename libiree-rocket-runtime<N> and
its .install file then. Within one SONAME, rocket/runtime.h only grows, so the
shlibs dependency is >= the upstream version and there is no symbols file.

The headers sit one directory down, under /usr/include/iree-rocket-runtime/,
because they include all of IREE's runtime headers. In /usr/include they
would collide file for file with any other IREE. pkg-config and the CMake
package add the right -I.

The Rust crates in rocket-runtime/rust are not packaged. rocket-runtime-sys
finds the system library through pkg-config, so `cargo build` against an
installed libiree-rocket-runtime-dev needs no configuration.

Not packaged here, on purpose
-----------------------------

  - Device access. Debian 13's systemd already gives /dev/accel/* to group
    render, mode 0660 (50-udev-default.rules), so no udev rule is shipped.
    Users who run models on the NPU need to be in the render group.

  - The kernel side. The measured results need three patches to the rocket
    module (kernel-patches/), and the 600 MHz NPU clock is a module option
    (options rocket rocket_npu_clk_hz=600000000) that only exists with patch
    081. Both belong in the kernel build (for Armbian, its kernel patch
    series), not in a userspace package. Stock modules still work, but
    pooling jobs are killed by the watchdog: see agent-docs/board-setup.md.

The orig tarball
----------------

debian/make-orig.sh builds iree-rocket_<upstream>.orig.tar.xz (about 3 GB
unpacked) from git objects. It contains:

  - this repository without debian/;
  - IREE at the recorded submodule commit, with only the five IREE
    submodules the builds read: llvm-project, torch-mlir (without its nested
    externals/), flatcc, benchmark and googletest. The runtime build needs
    only the last three;
  - every crate in Cargo.lock, vendored under vendor/.

debian/rules builds with `cargo --offline` whenever vendor/ is present, so
the build works with no network, as sbuild and Armbian's builders require.

  git submodule update --init iree-build/iree-src
  git -C iree-build/iree-src submodule update --init \
      third_party/{llvm-project,torch-mlir,flatcc,benchmark,googletest}
  debian/make-orig.sh                    # writes ../iree-rocket_*.orig.tar.xz

In a git worktree whose submodules are not initialized, point it at another
checkout with IREE_REPO=/path/to/iree-src. REV=$(git stash create) packages
uncommitted edits for a trial build.

Building
--------

The two halves build independently, and two build profiles pick one:

  pkg.iree-rocket.nocompiler   runtime packages only. No LLVM: minutes, not
                               hours. Armbian's arm64 builders want this.
  pkg.iree-rocket.noruntime    rocket-compiler only. The runtime is also
                               skipped automatically off arm64.

  dpkg-buildpackage -b                                       # everything
  dpkg-buildpackage -b -Ppkg.iree-rocket.nocompiler         # runtime only

A runtime-only build can also be cross-built from amd64. It needs a
build-arch IREE tools/ directory for the code generators (iree-flatcc-cli,
iree-c-embed-data) that the cross build cannot run, which any compiler-host
build has:

  IREE_HOST_BIN_DIR=/path/to/iree-build/build/tools \
    dpkg-buildpackage -a arm64 -b -Ppkg.iree-rocket.nocompiler,nocheck

The compiler cannot be cross-built (that would mean cross-building LLVM);
rules refuses to try.

Two shortcuts for the compiler that do not change the package's contents:

  ROCKET_IREE_BUILD_DIR=/path/to/iree-build/build
      Package an existing compiler-host build (lib/libIREECompiler.so and
      tools/iree-lld) instead of building IREE. This is the nightly CI's
      build. In a cross runtime build it also stands in for
      IREE_HOST_BIN_DIR.

  ROCKET_IREE_CMAKE_ARGS="-DCMAKE_C_COMPILER_LAUNCHER=ccache ..."
      Extra CMake arguments for the IREE configure steps.

What the build checks:

  - compiler: the unit tests, then one model compiled with the library being
    packaged. A spec the library cannot parse fails the build, not the user's
    first compile.
  - runtime (native builds only): a VMVX function
    (rocket-runtime/rust/rocket-runtime/tests/data/cpu_ops.vmvx.vmfb) run on
    the CPU device through rocket-run-module, statically and against the
    shared library. A buildd has no NPU.
  - SONAME: matches the library package's name.

The autopkgtests (debian/tests) repeat these against the installed packages.
They also run IREE's own runner and benchmark on the same module, and build
the shipped C++ sample through both pkg-config files and through
find_package(IreeRocketRuntime).

The Rust toolchain
------------------

The crates use edition 2024, so the build needs rustc >= 1.85. trixie has it.
bookworm, jammy and noble do not have one that new as their default rustc
(Ubuntu has versioned rustc-1.xx packages, which do not satisfy the
Build-Depends as written). On those, install Rust with rustup and build with
`dpkg-buildpackage -d` to skip the build-dependency check. A cross build
also needs the aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu Rust target.

One build per suite
-------------------

The binaries require the glibc and libstdc++ of the suite they were built
on. A build on noble (glibc 2.39) will not load on bookworm (2.36) or jammy
(2.35). Build once per target suite, in a container or chroot of that suite,
and publish each .deb to that suite's component of the apt repository.

Known lintian tags
------------------

  spelling-error-in-binary (libIREECompiler.so)    LLVM and IREE's strings;
                                                   overridden
  executable-in-usr-lib (iree-lld)                 private helper; overridden
  hardening-no-fortify-functions                   usual for Rust code
  debug-file-with-no-debug-symbols                 iree-lld (a 16 KB wrapper)
                                                   and rocket-run-module (C,
                                                   built without -g like all
                                                   the C and C++ here)
  package-has-long-file-name                       the snapshot version
                                                   string (git date, commit,
                                                   IREE version)
  no-symbols-control-file (libiree-rocket-runtime) deliberate; see above
  initial-upload-closes-no-bugs                    not aimed at Debian proper

